+* A restoration capability may suspend only the single frame that created it, only on its originating fiber, and only
+  while that frame is the topmost active mask frame.
+* A restoration attempted in the wrong nesting order, after its frame has become inactive, while the same frame is
+  already suspended, or on another fiber is an observational no-op.
+* Suspending a frame may reveal an older ordinary frame, an older uninterruptible frame, or the fiber's non-restorable
+  base state; it never removes or weakens any of those older states.
+* The frame is reinstated on every exit from the restored action before control continues outside that action.
+* If suspension reveals `Interruptible`, the transition immediately before the restored action is an interruption
+  point. Reinstatement immediately after a successful restored action is not preceded by an additional interruption
+  point. This prevents cancellation from consuming a completed result in the handoff back to masked code.
